"Positions a chain of user needs and supporting capabilities by visibility and market evolution to support strategic choices.\n\nUse when: Discuss build, buy and commoditization choices in context. Anticipate how evolving capabilities could change a value proposition.\n\nAvoid when: The team only needs a project timeline or a generic dependency graph.\n\nVisual grammar: User needs anchor the visible end of a dependency chain. The vertical dimension represents relative visibility to the user. The horizontal axis represents evolution from genesis through commodity.\n\nBuild: Identify the user and the need being served. Map necessary capabilities and their meaningful dependencies. Position each capability by evidenced evolution and examine strategic movement.\n\nCheck: Horizontal position reflects evolution rather than age or quality. Dependencies trace back to an actual user need. Proposed movements and current positions are visibly distinguishable.\n\nPitfalls: Treating evolution as a delivery schedule. Placing every newly adopted component in genesis despite a mature market.\n\nExample: A local delivery service maps parcel tracking, routing expertise and standard transport capacity to compare differentiation with purchasable utilities.\n\nAlternatives: Value chain, Business Model Canvas."
